mbuFormatTableHelp="
The disk formatting table contains a table of output partitions, 
with lines of the form:
    PARTITION dnum pnum fnc psize ptype
where
    dnum is the disk number (disks are numbered starting with 1)
    pnum is partition number to assign to the partition (a small integer >= 1)
        Maximum pnum is probably 16 ?
        For maximum sanity, list entries in order of pnum and avoid gaps
        in pnum for the same dnum.
    fnc is the function part of the partition name where the name
        will be generated as <set>-<fnc> (<set> will be provided later)
    psize is the integer partition size in BYTES;
        Unless it begins with + or - in which case it is passed to gdisk
        as is for end sector location... see gdisk man page.
        Otherwise, it may be an integer with suffix M for megabtyes
        (1024*1024 = mega) or G for gigabytes (1024*mega).
        It is advised to use M with megabytes since this will ensure that
